      "id": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/health/ap-report-fda-scientists-opposed-the-trump-administrations-move-to-ease-peptides-access",
      "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/health/ap-report-fda-scientists-opposed-the-trump-administrations-move-to-ease-peptides-access",
      "title": "HHS eases restrictions on peptide compounding despite gaps in human safety evidence",
      "content_html": "U.S. Health and Human Services Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. directed the Food and Drug Administration not to enforce certain rules against licensed pharmacies and physicians compounding six unapproved peptides, despite objections from FDA scientists who say evidence of their safety and effectiveness remains inadequate. The policy covers BPC-157, Epitalon, KPV, MOTS-c, Semax and TB-500; it does not give them FDA approval or establish that they are safe or effective.\n\nAn FDA advisory panel recommended allowing pharmacies and physicians to compound the six peptides in July, but FDA staff advised against the move. The substances have little or no human research, and the FDA had previously cited possible risks including allergic reactions, kidney damage and tumor growth. HHS says pharmacies must use traceable ingredients, conduct quality testing and report safety problems. The notice is scheduled for publication in the Federal Register on Oct. 30, with comments accepted through Nov. 13.",
